WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.646
Unwrap the unit-circle vertical coordinate as a function of angle

00:00:04.646 --> 00:00:13.137
Sine starts at zero, rises to one, returns through zero, reaches negative one, and completes its cycle at two pi

00:00:13.137 --> 00:00:16.533
The input axis here is measured in radians.

00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:25.742
Multiplying the output by two doubles every height without changing the input values where a cycle begins and ends

00:00:25.742 --> 00:00:29.904
The amplitude becomes two, while the period stays two pi

00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:34.067
Amplitude measures distance from the midline, not total peak-to-trough height.

00:00:35.367 --> 00:00:40.669
Multiplying the input by two makes the circular phase advance twice as quickly

00:00:40.669 --> 00:00:48.011
A full cycle now occurs while x advances only pi, so two cycles fit where one used to

00:00:48.011 --> 00:00:50.867
Input scaling changes period rather than amplitude.

00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:56.844
Adding one shifts every output upward, placing the midline at one

00:00:56.844 --> 00:01:03.223
The peaks are three and the troughs negative one, while amplitude and period stay unchanged

00:01:03.223 --> 00:01:07.900
Read vertical scale, input scale, and vertical shift as separate transformations.
